(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-endocrine-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Endocrine System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 2)","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is a PRIMARY (dedicated) endocrine organ rather than a tissue with a secondary endocrine role?","choices":["Heart","Thyroid gland","Kidney","Adipose tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Main endocrine organs include the pituitary, hypothalamus, thyroid, thymus, suprarenal, pineal, parathyroids, and pancreas; the heart, kidney, adipose, and digestive tract are 'other' endocrine tissues. — Slides 3–4 (Overview of the Endocrine System)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is attached to the hypothalamus by a stalk called the:","choices":["Optic chiasm","Infundibulum","Tuber cinereum","Cavernous sinus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The infundibulum connects the pituitary (hypophysis) to the hypothalamus; the gland sits in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica. — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The suprarenal (adrenal) gland is subdivided into which two parts?","choices":["Cortex and medulla","Anterior and posterior lobes","Follicular and parafollicular zones","Exocrine and endocrine acini"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each adrenal gland has an outer cortex and an inner medulla; the cortex has three zones and the medulla secretes catecholamines. — Slide 82 (The Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"Which pineal-gland hormone is listed in the endocrine overview?","choices":["Calcitonin","Melatonin","Aldosterone","Erythropoietin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 overview)"},{"q":"The parathyroid glands are typically located on the:","choices":["Anterior surface of the cricoid cartilage","Lateral wall of the carotid sheath","Superior pole of the thymus gland","Posterior surface of the thyroid gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior aspect of the thyroid, sometimes embedded within thyroid tissue. — Slide 43 (Parathyroid Glands, posterior view)"},{"q":"Which natriuretic peptides are secreted by the heart as an endocrine function?","choices":["T3 and T4","ANP and BNP","FSH and LH","EPO and calcitriol"],"answer":1,"exp":"The heart secretes atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP). — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 overview)"},{"q":"The kidney contributes to endocrine function by producing erythropoietin (EPO) and:","choices":["Cortisol","Calcitriol","Melatonin","Aldosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney produces EPO and calcitriol (active vitamin D). — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 overview)"},{"q":"The adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) releases how many peptide hormones, versus how many for the neurohypophysis?","choices":["Two anterior; nine posterior","Seven anterior; three posterior","Five anterior; five posterior","Nine anterior; two posterior"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adenohypophysis releases nine peptide hormones; the neurohypophysis releases two (oxytocin and ADH). — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"Which hormones are stored and released by the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary)?","choices":["Corticotropin and thyrotropin (ACTH and TSH)","Follicle-stimulating and luteinizing hormone (FSH/LH)","Growth hormone and prolactin from the acidophils","Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and ADH, which are actually produced in hypothalamic nuclei. — Slides 5, 10 (posterior pituitary hormones)"},{"q":"The structural/functional unit of the thyroid gland is the thyroid follicle, whose wall is made of which epithelium surrounding a central colloid?","choices":["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli","Simple cuboidal (follicular) epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium (duct-type)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each thyroid follicle is lined by simple cuboidal follicular epithelium enclosing colloid; active follicles have taller epithelium. — Slide 30 (Histology of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"Calcitonin-secreting cells of the thyroid, located near the follicular basement membrane, are the:","choices":["Zona glomerulosa cells","Principal (chief) cells","Parafollicular (C) cells","Follicular (colloid) cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parafollicular (C) cells secrete calcitonin and lie near the follicular basement lamina, larger and lighter-staining than follicular cells. — Slide 31 (Parafollicular/C cells)"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, the cells that stain with granules and secrete active hormones are the:","choices":["Chromophils (acidophils and basophils)","Chromophobes (granule-free cells)","Pituicytes (glial-type support cells)","Herring bodies (axonal storage sites)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chromophils have cytoplasmic granules: acidophils secrete GH and prolactin; basophils secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. Chromophobes lack granules. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"Acidophils of the anterior pituitary secrete which hormones?","choices":["Growth hormone and prolactin","Thyrotropin and corticotropin","Gonadotropins FSH and LH","Vasopressin and oxytocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Acidophils (reddish cytoplasm) secrete growth hormone and prolactin; basophils secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis cell types)"},{"q":"The parathyroid hormone (PTH)–secreting cells of the parathyroid gland are the:","choices":["Oxyphil (eosinophilic) cells","Parafollicular (C) cells","Principal (chief) cells","Zona glomerulosa cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chief (principal) cells produce PTH; oxyphil cells are larger, very eosinophilic, and of uncertain function (may also secrete PTH). — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"Which adrenal cortical zone secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone) and is composed of cells in ovoid clusters?","choices":["Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata","Zona glomerulosa","Adrenal medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"The zona glomerulosa (outer) secretes mineralocorticoids/aldosterone; fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids (cortisol); reticularis secretes androgens. — Slide 87 (Histology of adrenal glands)"},{"q":"The zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex is composed of lipid-laden cells in radial columns and secretes:","choices":["Mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)","Glucocorticoids (cortisol)","Sex steroids (androgens)","Catecholamines (epinephrine)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids such as cortisol. — Slide 87 (adrenal cortex zones)"},{"q":"The principal cells of the adrenal medulla, which secrete epinephrine and norepinephrine, are called:","choices":["Principal cells","Oxyphil cells","Chromaffin cells","Juxtaglomerular cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chromaffin cells — modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons lacking axons/dendrites — secrete adrenaline and noradrenaline; the medulla contains a large central vein. — Slide 89 (Adrenal medulla)"},{"q":"The endocrine cells of the pancreatic islets that secrete insulin are the:","choices":["Alpha cells","Delta cells","F cells","Beta cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Beta cells secrete insulin; alpha cells secrete glucagon; delta cells secrete somatostatin; F cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide. — Slide 52 (Histology of pancreatic islets)"},{"q":"In the pars nervosa, the supportive glial-like cells are called:","choices":["Chromaffin cells","Podocytes","Pituicytes","Chief cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pars nervosa contains nerve-fiber bundles and pituicytes; Herring bodies represent accumulated neurosecretory material. — Slide 22 (Pars nervosa)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland sits within the hypophyseal fossa of which bone?","choices":["Sphenoid bone","Ethmoid bone","Occipital bone","Temporal bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pituitary lies in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, covered superiorly by the diaphragma sellae. — Slide 13 (Pituitary relationships)"},{"q":"On each side, the pituitary gland is related to which venous structure?","choices":["Sigmoid sinus","Superior sagittal sinus","Cavernous sinus","Straight sinus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pituitary is related laterally to the cavernous sinus on each side, and superiorly the diaphragma sellae separates it from the optic chiasma. — Slide 13 (Pituitary relationships)"},{"q":"The anterior pituitary receives its blood supply mainly through which specialized vascular arrangement?","choices":["Direct arterial supply from the internal carotid","The hypophyseal portal system","The vertebral–basilar system","The cavernous venous plexus alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Regulatory hormones travel from the hypothalamus to the adenohypophysis through the hypophyseal portal veins; the posterior pituitary receives arterial blood. — Slides 17, 19 (Hypophyseal portal system)"},{"q":"The superior thyroid artery, supplying the thyroid gland, arises from the:","choices":["External carotid artery","Thyrocervical trunk","Arch of the aorta","Subclavian artery directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid; the inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The inferior thyroid artery arises from which vessel?","choices":["Superior thyroid branch of the external carotid","Costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery","Thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian artery","Internal thoracic branch of the subclavian artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk and becomes related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve at the posterior surface of the gland. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"On the medial surface of the thyroid, which nerve runs in the tracheoesophageal groove and is at risk during thyroid surgery?","choices":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve","External laryngeal nerve","Superior laryngeal nerve","Ansa cervicalis branches"],"answer":0,"exp":"The recurrent laryngeal nerve lies near the trachea/esophagus on the medial surface; the external laryngeal nerve accompanies the superior thyroid artery. — Slide 27 (Thyroid medial surface relations)"},{"q":"The inferior thyroid vein drains into which vein?","choices":["Internal jugular vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Subclavian vein","External jugular vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Superior and middle thyroid veins drain to the internal jugular; the inferior thyroid vein drains to the brachiocephalic vein. — Slide 33 (Venous drainage of thyroid)"},{"q":"The adrenal (suprarenal) glands receive arterial supply from three sources: the inferior phrenic artery, the renal artery, and the:","choices":["Testicular (gonadal) artery branch","Left gastric artery from the celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery branch","Middle suprarenal artery from the aorta"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior suprarenal (from inferior phrenic), middle suprarenal (from aorta), and inferior suprarenal (from renal artery) supply the adrenal gland. — Slides 81, 86 (adrenal blood supply)"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the adrenal glands is asymmetric: the RIGHT suprarenal vein drains to the IVC, while the LEFT drains to the:","choices":["Hepatic portal vein","Right azygos vein","Left splenic vein","Left renal vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right suprarenal vein drains directly to the IVC; the left suprarenal vein drains to the left renal vein. — Slide 86 (adrenal venous drainage)"},{"q":"The head of the pancreas — clinically important because ducts/vessels traverse it and carcinoma often arises there — fills the concavity of which structure?","choices":["The duodenum","The spleen","The stomach","The gallbladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreatic head fills the C of the duodenum; its blood supply comes from the pancreaticoduodenal arteries. — Slides 56, 65 (pancreas head relations)"},{"q":"The body and tail of the pancreas are supplied primarily by which artery?","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Left renal artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The splenic artery (a celiac branch) runs along the superior margin of the body and tail, supplying them; the head is supplied by pancreaticoduodenal arteries. — Slides 60, 70 (pancreas arterial supply)"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) joins the common bile duct and empties at the:","choices":["Ampulla of Vater (major duodenal papilla)","Minor duodenal papilla (accessory duct)","Pyloric sphincter of the stomach","Duodenojejunal flexure at the ligament of Treitz"],"answer":0,"exp":"The duct of Wirsung joins the CBD at the ampulla of Vater; the accessory duct of Santorini opens at the minor papilla. — Slides 71, 74 (pancreatic duct system)"},{"q":"The right adrenal gland's anterior relations include the liver and which vessel?","choices":["Inferior vena cava","Abdominal aorta","Left splenic vein","Hepatic portal vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right (pyramidal) adrenal is related anteriorly to the liver and IVC; the left (semilunar) adrenal relates to the stomach and pancreas. — Slide 86 (adrenal relations table)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is related inferiorly and anteriorly to which air-filled space?","choices":["The frontal air sinus","The ethmoid air cells","The maxillary sinus cavity","The sphenoid air sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Inferiorly and in front, the pituitary is related to the sphenoid air sinus; behind it lies the dorsum sellae. — Slide 13 (Pituitary relationships)"},{"q":"The thyroid isthmus typically lies anterior to which tracheal rings?","choices":["2nd and 3rd tracheal rings","1st tracheal ring only","5th and 6th tracheal rings","Cricoid cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The isthmus lies opposite the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings, covered by the sternohyoid and sternothyroid muscles. — Slide 29 (Thyroid isthmus)"},{"q":"The parathyroid glands share their blood, lymphatic, and nerve supply with the:","choices":["Larynx only","Thyroid gland","Cervical trachea","Cervical esophagus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parathyroids' blood, lymphatic, and nerve supply are the same as for the thyroid gland; they lie between the posteromedial thyroid lobes and the carotid sheath. — Slide 47 (Parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"The uncinate process of the pancreas lies posterior to which vessels?","choices":["The abdominal aorta and inferior vena cava","The hepatic portal vein and proper hepatic artery","The splenic artery and vein along the pancreatic body","The superior mesenteric artery and vein (SMA and SMV)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uncinate process lies posterior to the SMA and SMV and anterior to the aorta. — Slide 57 (pancreas uncinate relations)"},{"q":"Which hormone LOWERS blood calcium and is secreted by the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid?","choices":["Parathyroid hormone (PTH)","Calcitriol","Calcitonin","Aldosterone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Calcitonin, from thyroid C cells, lowers blood calcium; it opposes PTH, which raises calcium. — Slides 5, 31 (calcitonin from C cells)"},{"q":"Which hormone RAISES blood calcium and is produced by the chief cells of the parathyroid glands?","choices":["Calcitonin (thyroid C cells)","Parathyroid hormone (PTH)","Insulin from beta cells","Thyroxine (T4) from follicles"],"answer":1,"exp":"PTH, secreted by parathyroid chief cells, raises blood calcium — the principal calcium-elevating hormone. — Slides 5, 46 (PTH from chief cells)"},{"q":"The active form of vitamin D that participates in calcium metabolism, produced by the kidney, is:","choices":["Calcitonin","Cortisol","Calcitriol","Erythropoietin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The kidney produces calcitriol (active vitamin D), which promotes intestinal calcium absorption and works with PTH in calcium homeostasis. — Slide 5 (kidney — calcitriol)"},{"q":"Calcitonin and PTH are described as having what relationship in calcium regulation?","choices":["They are antagonistic — PTH raises and calcitonin lowers blood calcium","They are synergistic — both PTH and calcitonin raise blood calcium levels","They are antagonistic — calcitonin raises and PTH lowers blood calcium","They are unrelated — neither hormone affects blood calcium regulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"PTH (calcium-raising) and calcitonin (calcium-lowering) act antagonistically to regulate blood calcium levels. — Slides 5, 31, 46 (calcium regulation)"},{"q":"Because the parathyroid glands are the chief source of PTH, inadvertent removal during thyroid surgery would most directly impair:","choices":["Blood glucose regulation","Erythrocyte production","Sodium/water balance","Blood calcium regulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Loss of PTH from the parathyroids would impair calcium regulation, since PTH is the primary calcium-elevating hormone. — Slides 43–46 (Parathyroid glands / PTH)"},{"q":"Which cells are the calcium-related hormone source located ON the posterior thyroid, distinct from the calcitonin-producing cells embedded WITHIN the thyroid follicular tissue?","choices":["Zona glomerulosa cells vs. zona fasciculata cells of the adrenal cortex","Parathyroid chief cells (PTH) vs. thyroid C cells (calcitonin)","Pancreatic alpha islet cells vs. beta islet cells of Langerhans","Anterior pituitary acidophils vs. basophils of the pars distalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parathyroid chief cells (on the posterior thyroid) secrete PTH, while parafollicular C cells (within the thyroid) secrete calcitonin — the two opposing calcium hormones. — Slides 31, 43, 46 (C cells vs. chief cells)"},{"q":"Which gland undergoes progressive atrophy during adulthood?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Pancreas","Pineal gland","Thymus gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thymus undergoes atrophy during adulthood, per the endocrine overview. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 overview)"},{"q":"The hypothalamus exerts endocrine control via three mechanisms, including releasing ADH and oxytocin to the pituitary and controlling which structure via sympathetic output?","choices":["The thyroid follicular cells","The pineal gland's melatonin output","The parathyroid chief cells","The suprarenal (adrenal) medulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hypothalamus acts as an endocrine organ, releases ADH/oxytocin to the pituitary, and controls the adrenal medulla via sympathetic (preganglionic) fibers. — Slide 9 (Hypothalamus and Endocrine Regulation)"},{"q":"Anatomically, the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is subdivided into the pars distalis, pars tuberalis, and:","choices":["Pars intermedia","Pars nervosa","Median eminence","Infundibulum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adenohypophysis comprises the pars distalis, pars tuberalis, and pars intermedia; the neurohypophysis is the pars nervosa. — Slide 14 (pituitary subdivisions)"},{"q":"The gonads function as endocrine organs: the testes secrete androgens and inhibin, while the ovaries secrete estrogens, progestins, and:","choices":["Calcitonin","Inhibin","Aldosterone","Erythropoietin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Both gonads secrete inhibin; testes add testosterone/androgens and ovaries add estrogens and progestins. — Slide 5 (gonads overview)"},{"q":"The colloid stored in the center of each thyroid follicle serves to:","choices":["Filter plasma proteins from the bloodstream","Generate red blood cells via erythropoiesis","Produce calcitonin for calcium regulation","Store thyroid hormone precursor (thyroglobulin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Follicular colloid stores the thyroid-hormone-containing material; active follicles show tall epithelium as hormone is mobilized. — Slides 30, 40 (thyroid follicles/colloid)"},{"q":"Chromophobes make up roughly what fraction of pars distalis cells and characteristically LACK cytoplasmic granules?","choices":["About 10%","About 1%","About 90%","About 50%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Chromophobes are ~50% of pars distalis cells and have no granules; the other ~50% are chromophils (acidophils + basophils). — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"Basophils of the anterior pituitary secrete all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Thyrotropin (TSH)","Corticotropin (ACTH)","Growth hormone","FSH and LH hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Basophils secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH; growth hormone is secreted by acidophils. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis cell types)"},{"q":"Oxyphil cells of the parathyroid gland are best described as:","choices":["Larger, very eosinophilic cells of uncertain function","Small, basophilic cells with numerous secretory granules","Cuboidal, colloid-storing follicular cells of the thyroid","Chromaffin, catecholamine-secreting cells of the medulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxyphil cells are larger and strongly eosinophilic with an unknown function (may secrete PTH); chief cells are the main PTH producers. — Slide 46 (parathyroid histology)"},{"q":"The parathyroid glands frequently contain increasing amounts of which tissue in older individuals?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Cartilage","Adipose tissue","Colloid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Adipose tissue is frequently found in the parathyroid gland in older individuals. — Slide 46 (parathyroid histology)"},{"q":"The zona reticularis of the adrenal cortex is a loose meshwork of cells that secretes:","choices":["Aldosterone","Cortisol","Epinephrine","Androgens"],"answer":3,"exp":"The innermost zona reticularis secretes androgens; glomerulosa secretes aldosterone and fasciculata secretes cortisol. — Slide 87 (adrenal cortex zones)"},{"q":"The thyroid gland moves up and down with swallowing (deglutition) because it is enclosed within the:","choices":["Carotid sheath","Prevertebral fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thyroid lies within the pretracheal fascia, which attaches above to the thyroid cartilage, so the gland ascends with swallowing. — Slide 28 (thyroid fascia)"},{"q":"The thyroidea ima artery, an uncommon variant supplying the thyroid isthmus, typically arises from the arch of the aorta or the:","choices":["Superior thyroid (external carotid) artery","Vertebral artery of the neck","Brachiocephalic (innominate) artery","Facial branch of the external carotid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thyroidea ima is a small, uncommon artery supplying the isthmus, arising from the aortic arch or brachiocephalic artery. — Slide 32 (thyroid blood supply)"},{"q":"The carotid sheath, which delineates the lateral aspect of the thyroid, contains the internal jugular vein, the vagus nerve, and the:","choices":["Common and internal carotid arteries","Recurrent laryngeal nerve branches","Phrenic nerve and cervical plexus","Thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian"],"answer":0,"exp":"The carotid sheath contains the internal jugular vein (lateral), the common/internal carotid arteries, and the vagus nerve. — Slide 38 (carotid sheath)"},{"q":"The splenic vein, which runs on the posterior surface of the pancreas, terminates in the:","choices":["Inferior vena cava","Portal vein","Left renal vein","Azygos vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The splenic vein runs parallel to the splenic artery on the posterior pancreas and terminates in the portal vein. — Slide 62 (splenic vein)"},{"q":"The superior and inferior mesenteric veins pass deep to the pancreas and merge with the splenic vein to terminate in the:","choices":["Renal vein","Hepatic vein","Portal vein","Azygos vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The SMV and IMV pass deep to the pancreas, merge with the splenic vein, and terminate in the portal vein. — Slide 63 (mesenteric veins)"},{"q":"The pancreas lies retroperitoneally in the epigastric and left hypochondriac regions at approximately which vertebral level?","choices":["T9 through T11","S1 through S3","L4 through L5","T12/L1 to L3"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreas lies retroperitoneally at ~T12/L1 to L3. — Slide 56 (pancreas location)"},{"q":"The body of the pancreas is related posteriorly to the left crus of the diaphragm, the left adrenal, the left renal vein, and the:","choices":["Portal vein","Splenic vein","Superior mesenteric artery only","Gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The body of the pancreas relates posteriorly to the left crus, left adrenal, left renal vein, and splenic vein; the celiac trunk lies superior to the body. — Slide 59 (pancreas body relations)"},{"q":"The pancreatic head derives its blood supply from the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; the superior branches originate from the gastroduodenal artery (GDA) and the inferior from the:","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)","Splenic artery via pancreatic branches","Left gastric artery (celiac branch)","Inferior mesenteric artery branch"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anterior/posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcades form from GDA (superior) and SMA (inferior) branches, giving the head an extensive dual supply. — Slides 67–69 (pancreaticoduodenal arteries)"}]};
